8.7295,13.7956],[28.7569,14.4603]]}],"labels":[{"at":[9.5856,7.3933],"text":"$r = R$","anchor":"l","class":"small","dx":10,"dy":0,"ring":{"surface":0,"ring":2,"side":1}},{"at":[19.1712,11.4839],"text":"$2R$","anchor":"l","class":"small","dx":8,"dy":0,"ring":{"surface":0,"ring":3,"side":1}},{"at":[28.7569,14.4603],"text":"$3R$","anchor":"l","class":"small","dx":8,"dy":0,"ring":{"surface":0,"ring":4,"side":1}},{"at":[-2.8003,0.0],"text":"$r_s$","anchor":"r","class":"small","dx":-8,"dy":0,"ring":{"surface":0,"ring":5,"side":-1}}],"legend":[["fill","star","the star, where $p > 0$"],["fill","cover","the exterior, Flamm's paraboloid of the star's mass"],["line","r","$r$ constant, at $R/3$ and $2R/3$ inside and $2R$ and $3R$ outside"],["line","surface","the surface of the star, $r = R$, where the pressure falls to zero"],["line","reference","the vacuum paraboloid inside $R$, down to its throat at $r_s = 2GM/c^2$"],["line","meridian","$\\phi$ constant, every $15°$"]],"turn":{"origins":[[0.0,0.0]],"meridians":24,"tint":{"star":"star","sheet":"cover"},"marks":[]}},"settings":"$G = c = M_\\odot = 1$, so that the unit of every length is $GM_\\odot/c^2 = 1.48$ km.","input":"A polytrope, $p = K\\rho_0^2$ with rest mass density $\\rho_0$ and energy density $\\rho c^2$, where $\\rho = \\rho_0 + p/c^2$, at $K = 100$ and a central $\\rho_0 = 1.28\\times10^{-3}$, solved from this spacetime's own $G^t{}_t$ and $G^r{}_r$: a star of $M = 1.40\\,M_\\odot$ and $R = 14.2$ km, the star on which numerical relativists test their codes.","caption":["The equatorial plane ($\\theta = \\pi/2$) of a neutron star at one moment of $t$, drawn as a surface in flat space with every distance along it the metric distance. On it $g_{rr} = r/(r - 2m)$, with $m = GM(r)/c^2$ for the mass $M(r)$ inside $r$, so the surface climbs at $dz/dr = \\sqrt{2m/(r - 2m)}$: level at the centre, where $m$ grows as $r^3$, and steeper outward as the mass inside grows. Outside the star $m$ no longer grows, and the surface is Flamm's paraboloid for the star's mass.","The curvature of the surface at radius $r$ is $4\\pi G(\\rho - \\bar\\rho/3)/c^2$, with $\\bar\\rho$ the mean density inside $r$. At the centre the two are equal and the surface curves like a cap. In the outer layers the density falls below a third of the mean and the surface curves like a saddle, as Flamm's paraboloid does everywhere, while Karl Schwarzschild's star of uniform density is a cap of a sphere all the way out. This star, at $2GM/c^2R = 0.29$, ends far above the throat the vacuum paraboloid would have at $r_s = 2GM/c^2$, drawn dashed below it."]}]}
